<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Runtime directives for the .NET Native toolchain, which compiles this project in Release only.
  Debug builds run on CoreCLR and ignore this file entirely - so nothing here can be verified by
  building Debug, and its absence cannot be noticed there either. That is exactly how it went
  missing: the project was authored by hand rather than from the VS template, and the template's
  copy of this file is the only reason most UWP apps have one.

  .NET Native compiles ahead of time and discards metadata it cannot prove is needed. Reflection
  is what it cannot prove, and the failure mode is a MissingMetadataException at the moment of the
  reflecting call - not at build time. In a Game Bar widget that surfaces as a card that never
  populates, with no console and no crash dialog to read.

  The one call that makes this load-bearing is in Ipc/HelperConnection.cs:

      if (!Enum.IsDefined(typeof(Function), fnId)) continue;

  That gates EVERY message arriving from the helper. Enum.IsDefined needs the enum's field
  metadata; without it the call throws rather than returning false, and the widget goes silent
  across all six features at once while the helper log shows it sending perfectly good frames.
  McenterLite.Shared also reaches Enum.GetNames/Enum.Parse through a generic parameter
  (LightingProfile.TryParseEnum<T>), which is the form the toolchain is least able to reason about.

  "*Application*" is the toolchain's wildcard for the assemblies in this package that are not
  framework assemblies - here McenterLite.Widget and McenterLite.Shared. Rooting them costs some
  package size and compile time and buys immunity from the whole class of problem, which is the
  right trade for six features whose only IPC is enum-keyed.
-->
<Directives xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/netfx/2013/01/metadata">
  <Application>
    <Assembly Name="*Application*" Dynamic="Required All" />
  </Application>
</Directives>
